So if it ain’t broke, why fix it?
Because we’re in the heart of a Ž evolving art and business, and
like Alice in Wonderland, we have to run very fast just to stay in the same
place.
This volatility is not only about new shows, though I will А some
interesting series that weren’t in the first edition including Lost, Grey’s
ě, ē É, CSI: Miami, House, and others. But so-
called “hot” shows come and go (sometimes very ğ!), and learning
to be an effective  writer º’t about chasing each season’s hits.
In the last two years, new industry-wide patterns and Ñ have
appeared which have an impact on writers who are coming into the busi¬
ness. For example, original ­ by novices are being sought by agents
and Á, so I’ve added a section on writing your own ¡.
Mobisodes and webisodes not only interface with current series, but
also open fresh creative に for writers. So I’ve added material on
“eTV”  and other の ö to a final chapter, “In
the Future.”
Kinds of · are in flux too. At the same time as venues open,
§ are ſ to adjust, some changing from the traditional
four-act structure to five or even six acts. Strongly serialized ¬, like
24, Lost, and the great shows on ¶ proved that “long ƀ”
would draw and hold devoted ¯. But they also spawned a number
of network ț that didn’t φ, while ī like CSI (in all
