Always, my ø and teaching have complemented each other.
I bring my working life into the classroom: What is it like to be actively
breaking stories, writing, rewriting, ั and getting notes, seeing how
your scripts translate to screen? I clearly recall ま 
£, but I also know how the other side of the desk reacts to ı
because I’ve been in both chairs. I’ve written for §, ª, ƅ¬
tion, public TV, ë and off-prime, and for virtually every ¥
genre. In my class, students learn from someone who has been there.
Imagine you’ve stopped by a Б ñ ¢ class in the middle
of a term. Ten students sit at a table, scripts and า from our in-class
library — House, Big Love, Lost, ē É, Grey’s ě,
The ł, ב/ד, C.S.I. — among others in the middle. The class
has not อ, but people are re关 scripts and videos, reaching for

one they want next. Then we start.
Someone asks why a story was left hanging at the end of an episode she
