more than you admit; you even talk about them when they’re not around
— after all, they come every week. Sometimes they’re broiling over issues
in the news. Or sick and ア about that, or lying, or brave. At one time
they were attacked and fought back and barely ƛ. But no matter
what, they’ll be back next week, your same friends, there with you in your
most vulnerable place, at home when you’re tired after work. Intimate.
Maybe one of them is Tony Ɯ, the mob boss, asking Uncle Junior,
“I thought you loved me,” and â Junior’s lip quiver, unable to
answer. Or β Andy   of ² Blue, just Ɲ with
his adult son, lifting the sheet off a corpse, to discover his son. On West
ə, you were Д by a swell of ィ when reason þ
in a hard-won Supreme Court appointment. You were drawn into the
D.A.’s イ ƞ when an old man can’t be stopped from Ɵ
revenge against a Ơ who took his house, on Law and Order. And
you couldn’t resist rooting for the nurses on ER in the midst of Ƣ
as they dealt with an ƣ patient by pushing his Ƥ in the hall and
“taking his temperature” with a plastic flower in his rear. Joy and tears up
close and personal.
