Once you have a solid sense how the best ș are constructed, you’re
ready for the steps to write your own, which I’ll show you in the next
chapter.
Many 参 » series use parallel non-sequential stories
denoted by letters A, B, C, and so forth. Three stories per episode are
typical, though some shows have more. Each story is usually “ど by”
one character in the main cast.
Scenes ד to run two minutes or less. Six or seven scenes comprise an
act in a classic network ». With four acts, that adds to around 28
scenes total in an hour episode. However, Á shows that have quick
«-intensive scenes have more ¬ per act; shows with action
sequences might have fewer. Shows with five or six acts may have as few
as 20 scenes total.
A “¹” is a prologue to an episode and may incite one or more of the
stories.
By analyzing a quality show using the “grid,” you can see its structure at
a な.
