HOW TO WRITE YOUR OUTLINE
Nobody launches into an outline at number one and trucks on to number
28 in a straight line. Instead, start with the tent-プ I mentioned and
fill in the grid until it’s complete ­ to write the scenes as an outline.
Or you could begin with index cards, as many writers do. Cards are
ț because they’re not threatening. If you don’t like an idea, toss
it out. If your order doesn’t make sense, re-arrange the cards. And you can
“down-load” your thoughts in any order, which lets you reach creaरly
to scenes you’d enjoy whether or not they fit next in the script.
One technique is to choose different color cards for each story. If you do
all the ¬ of the yellow story, then the green one, then the blue, and
assemble them, you’ll see at a な whether you’ve lost your green story
in the second act, and whether the blue is ผ off in Act Four or if it
ǐ out in Act Three (which might be okay, but at least you’ll have a
Б to ask the question).
Remember, in a multiple-story show, the stories are probably uneven. So,
if you’ll have around 28 scenes in an hour episode, and your “A” story
Ǒ, it might have as many as 12 to 16 ¬; the “B” could
have eight to 12 ¬ or so, and the “C” has whatever is left, as few as
three or four ¬, up to maybe eight. Of course, if you’re Ђ with
ǒ writing, with seven or eight parallel stories, you might have a
whole little tale in a single beat, though that’s not typical of most ș.
Understand, I’m not ɮting exact numbers of scenes that should go
with any story. I’m illustrating the kind of ı you might do.
