How close should you stay to your outline? That depends how close your
outline is to what works on screen. If you’re on an assignment from a show,
you will have vetted your outline with the head writer ™, so you’re sent off to your first draft with an implied contract
to deliver what they expect. Sometimes an outline is ป “locked,”
which means you’re committed to the ¬ on the page and you’d better
stick to them. In hasty or ultra-low budget producェ, some companies
have been known to start prepping (pre-production) based on the outline.
(That may mean scouting locaェ and rough 主, for example.)
But what if you come upon something you want to fix? Say, in the outline,
a beat exists to reveal a character’s secret, but while writing, you realize the
secret is already apparent from a previous scene, so you need to cut the extra
beat. Or you might want to make a β change: The guest cast pops out,
ア in a way that’s more interesting than appeared in the outline, and the
“voice” of the character demands that Á scenes be angled ه.
If you’re doing a spec, absolutely go for the revi✔s if you’re sure of
them. You don’t get points for sticking ǩ to an outline that doesn’t
I 4 R W R I I I N B THE TV D R H M fl § / P R M 0 0 U B L R S
make sense! But if this script is for a producer, it’s better not to make large
changes without asking. I made that ו once. I was doing a script for
a show and had Ǫ worked out my outline with the show-runner.
But as I נed Act Four, I was inspired by what I thought was a
more バ resolution, so I went ahead and ³ it.
Well, one day after I delivered the script, the producer was on the phone
ǫ I 么’t given him the ending we’d П. Ǭ by
the ç tenor of his reaction, I グ silently as he went on about
this single point before I ǭ what he was really ñ. The orig¬
inal ending had been his idea. Whether or not my ver✔ was better, his
て were hurt — not just because I 么’t used his ɮtion, but
because he felt I’d Ǯ him. This was about respect. Aha, I made a
mental note: In the future, pick up the phone and ask. If you get the boss
on board, he’ll probably say okay to write what you think is best.
Now, how do you actually do the script? You’ve written יs before
or you İ’t have จ this point, and this one is not so different.
Once you get past the structural requirements of the hour format, and
you’ve Â your stories via the show’s à cast, the next special
factor is speed. Episodes are usually due two weeks after the outline is
approved, and that feels fast if you’re used to mulling over a feature for
months. Of course, if you’re ‎, no one will know how long you
took to write, but a ǯ schedule is a habit you’ll need if you’re
going to work in ¨.
I’ll show you how easy it is to deliver in 14 days. Let’s say you have an
outline with 28 ¬ ز. I like
to follow my outline ó, so I write just two scenes each day. Voila! 14
X 2 = 28. When I’m writing at home Ā, I begin each ω
reading over what I ³ the day before, fine-tuning it. Then I take a
ท and get ready for the first scene of the day. I נ it as if these
next pages are the single most important piece of writing I’ll ever do. I
want to bring to this screen moment the richest experience, full of subtext
and nuance, while delivering the action in the tightest way I can. I might
‐ the whole scene before writing a word, or take a walk and jot
down ideas, or close my eyes and wait for the characters’ voices. Whatever
it takes. Then I write two or three pages. And stop.
I find that pushing on ǰ quality, and I want to come to the next
scene fresh. So I’ll take a break. Lunch, errands, gym, calls — I try to take
my mind off it, though when I’m most relaxed, not even trying. I’ll have
  YOUR OWN E P I 5 0 D E I 4 9
ideas for a way into the next scene or a ら on one I ³. Much
later in the day, in the ё or night, I’ll re-read the ω’s scene
and revise it. Then I repeat the process of finding, forming, and writing the
second scene of the day. And stop.
I’ve found that by the time I reach the end of the script, my first draft has
already been edited because I refine my work each day. Of course, not
everyone works like this, nor should they. I have a writer friend who starts
work at 4:00 A.M. and smashes through as many pages as she can before
she runs out of steam, hardly looking back. She р me she’d never let
В see that “mess” that rambles, repeats, and wanders into 义.
She regards it as raw material which she edits away after she ́ at the
end. You could think of it as the difference between 之 and carving
a Ǳ: The painter pays attention to each brush stroke, adding one
after another until the picture is formed. The シ ð with a hunk
of material, and cuts away “everything that ß’t the サ,” to para¬
phrase Michaelangelo. One method ß’t better than another — whatever
works for you is right.
If you’re ǲ by a blank page, put something on it — anything. A
之 司er once taught me that as I stood staring at a blank canvas.
He 也 over and threw ink on my pristine surface, and that got me
moving, even if only to clean up the ink. Some writers break the empti¬
ness with automatic writing or anything a character might say, even if
it’s not the way to open the scene. Some people write by hand for the
ǳ feel of words flowing from the mind onto paper. Others talk into
a tape recorder.
