Remember, you’re writing a “selling script,” not a “г script.” You
want to entice a reader, especially if this is a spec, so write what will keep
someone interested. Mr. Р shared this أ at the Seminar:
“...The selling script is the most important right now. I’m not writing a script right
now for a line producer to sit and budget, for a DP [Director of Photography] to
work at. I’m writing a script for you to read, to sit there at night — you can’t stop
έ the pages, this is so much fun. Even now in the scripts that I write, I only,
事, describe what’s important for you to get that moment. It’s possible that
I’m going to describe, ‘and the camera 二 in and 二 in and 二 in’
and I’m probably going to write it like that because I’m building û for the

reader at that point. Mostly I write for «, and « is what you read
fastest when you’re reading a י. Description just slows it down...”
Aim А page 17 to end your first act س, page 30 to end Act Two, around 45 to end Act Three, and ็
between 55 and 65 at the end of Act Four (57 to 60 pages is an average
total length). Those are approximate guides, not rules, though. A filmed
episode might run 串 from 44 to 52 minutes Ą,
depending on the outlet, but you won’t know the actual length of your script
until ã η from now when a г script is read through by the
cast and timed with a stopwatch. I บ you that page count only so you can
check î. If you’re way off — for example an hour script that’s 30 pages
